es are going  to deteriorate they're going to  deteriorate further and that we need to  get used to that there's a new normal  and that Baseline of what we expect from  the Health Service is now lower those  are the two options for the government  what they're doing right now which is  letting inflation erode wages but still  saying Services can be maintained that  just from an economic perspective is  plainly dishonest okay and they argue  that they're putting lots of money into  the NHS don't they if the isn't a  resolution do you expect to see more  strikes to come the RCN it is saying  that that is the case if there aren't  negotiations and there isn't progress we  can expect more after these the ones  next week I think that's right I think  there will be more strikes if the  government continues with its line I  suspect those strikes that come in  January will be more severe and more  coordinated and will lead to Greater  disruptions so I think I mean the offer  the RCN has made to the government which  is if you sit at the table we will  suspend the strikes that feels I think  to most people like a pretty reasonable  offer to have made but if the government  continues to say no we're saying  Hardball then I suspect the strikes to  come will be more disruptive what about  patient safety do you have concerns you  work in a e yourself I know you haven't  worked this week so you can't comment on  what effect the strike had yesterday but  do you have worries that during strikes  Emergency Care is compromised  right I mean there's there's sort of two  ways to think about how the strikes  affect patient care there's what it  means for patients in the immediate term  what it meant for patients yesterday  what it will mean for patients next  Tuesday next Wednesday and then there's  what it means for patients over the  coming months and years so let's take  each of those in turn what does it mean  for patients in the immediate term have  Services being disrupted yes tha